INT. MUNICIPAL CLOCK TOWER, MECHANISM ROOM - NIGHT

Brass wheels the size of wagon tires turn in the dark, each tooth polished by a century of oil. A catwalk skirts the gears. Below, behind a steel grate, the vault of the Merchants' Exchange sleeps.

SILAS "SPARROW" OKOYE-BRANTLEY (40s, silver mustache, gloves the color of old cream) works a hand drill into the vault grate with the patience of a watchman. Beside him, a canvas roll of tools. Behind him, the great pendulum swings.

SPARROW
(to the gears)
Three and a half minutes, old girl. Don't fail me now.

The drill bites through. He blows the dust away and lifts the grate. He smiles.

Footsteps on the stair. Quick, light. Sparrow freezes, drops the drill, and slides a hand toward his coat.

PATRICIA "PATSY" VELLACOTT (30s, bobbed wig, a pearl-handled revolver at her hip) appears on the landing, unhurried, and gives him a small wave.

SPARROW (CONT'D)
Patsy! You were meant to be at the bank.

PATSY
The bank was dull, Sparrow. You were slowing.

SPARROW
I'm precise. It is a different thing.

PATSY
The watchman makes his round in four minutes.

SPARROW
Then we have three and a half.

She crouches beside the open grate and peers into the dark.

PATSY
Sparrow, you beautiful old crook.

SPARROW
Flattery will cost you ten percent.

The pendulum swings. The great clock begins to chime midnight, deafening in the cramped room. They freeze.

PATSY
(shouting over it)
That's not on the schedule!

SPARROW
(shouting back)
Nothing is on the schedule, Patsy!

Below, in the vault, a light snaps on. A heavy door opens. Boots.

PATSY
Sparrow, tell me that's the watchman.

SPARROW
It's the watchman.

PATSY
You're lying.

SPARROW
Yes.

She draws the pearl-handled revolver at last, grinning like a girl at a fair.

PATSY
Ten percent off the top, then.

SPARROW
Naturally. Run.
